// Decollement / subduction-interface surfaces. Depth is km BELOW SEA LEVEL (absolute).
// Both trenches come from ONE published model (Slab2, Hayes et al. 2018) plus the same
// user-supplied trench shapefile, so the interface geometry needs a single citation.
// Both grids are 0.02 deg (Manila is Slab2 native; Ryukyu is resampled from its native 0.05 deg).
// depth[j][i] <-> lats[j], lons[i]; null = outside the model.
// src[j] is a per-row string: "0" = Slab2, "1" = interpolated -- either up-dip from the trench
// axis to the shallowest reliable Slab2 contour (10 km), or across an interior hole in the grid.
// trench is [lon, lat, seabedDepthKm] -- the full mapped axis at its GEBCO_2026 seabed depth,
// drawn untrimmed even where it runs past the interface.
// edge / deepEdge are one [lon, lat, depthKm] per grid row (edgeAxis "lat") or column ("lon"):
// the up-dip boundary (on the trench axis) and the down-dip 60 km cut-off.
// The grids are trimmed to the span of the trench axis: a lane beyond it would have no up-dip
// vertex, so its polyline would start at the first grid cell and the boundary would step by a
// whole band width. Extending the axis instead would mean inventing trench geometry.
